Chapter 01 Initial Assessment and the Deteriorating Patient
Key points

Rule: Assess → treat → reassess → decide level of care. Do not wait for a complete diagnosis if vital functions are threatened.

Link anatomy to function: Airway = open/protected; Breathing = oxygenation/ventilation; Circulation = perfusion/cardiac output; Disability = consciousness/glucose; Exposure = cause + temperature/privacy.

Oxygen: usually 94–98%; if risk of CO2 retention, target 88–92%. Do not delay rescue oxygen in severe hypoxia.

Fluids: match type/volume to shock mechanism; reassess continuously.

Hypoglycemia in adults: treat if glucose <4.0 mmol/L. If safe swallow: 15–20 g oral glucose. If IV: 200 mL 10% glucose or 100 mL 50% glucose over 15 min; recheck after 10 min. No IV: glucagon 1 mg IM; less effective in liver disease/glycogen depletion.

Memorize traps: normal BP does not rule out shock; good saturation does not rule out hypoventilation; initial normal Hb does not rule out bleeding.

Define the decision before the answer: diagnosis? first action? definitive treatment? admission/discharge? Ask what factor would make the alternative correct.

Chapter 02 Cardiac Arrest and Post Resuscitation Decisions
Key points

Sequence: recognize cardiac arrest → start resuscitation → classify shockable/non-shockable → treat reversible causes → protect organs after ROSC.

Shockable: VF / pulseless VT. Non-shockable: PEA / asystole. Do not shock asystole/PEA.

UK adult pathway: epinephrine 1 mg every 3–5 min; amiodarone 300 mg after 3rd shock, then 150 mg after 5 shocks. These are cardiac arrest doses, not for a pulse.

Reversible causes: hypoxia, hypovolemia, hypo/hyperkalemia, hypothermia, thrombosis, tension pneumothorax, tamponade, toxins, coronary occlusion.

Post-ROSC UK: SpO2 94–98%, PaCO2 35–45 mmHg; avoid hypotension and fever. Selected algorithm: SBP >100 mmHg, MAP >65 mmHg.

Comatose post-ROSC: avoid fever; target ≤37.5°C for at least 72 h per mentioned pathway.

Neuro-prognostication deferred at least 72 h; do not rely on one sign.

AHA 2025 comparison: SpO2 90–98%, temperature control 32–37.5°C for no command response, at least 36 h. Do not mix protocols.

Persistent VF: DSED is rescue option, not universal; continue good CPR and correct causes.

Thrombolysis after 60–90 min is not universal; keep UK pathway for suspected PE arrest.

Chapter 03 Airway Oxygenation and Ventilatory Failure
OSCE sudden ventilator-associated collapse

Intubated obstructive lung disease + hypotension + rising airway pressures: reassess patient and airway/equipment immediately; differentiate tube/equipment failure, dynamic hyperinflation/auto-PEEP, tension pneumothorax.

Do not immediately increase pressure/rate. Call airway/critical care help. Check tube position/patency, circuit, oxygen supply, breath sounds, circulation.

Compare peak vs plateau pressure: peak only = high resistance; peak + plateau = low compliance. Check expiratory flow for air trapping; review rate, tidal volume, inspiratory time, synchrony.

If unstable tension physiology: treat urgently without waiting for chest X-ray. Do not delay rescue.

Trap: rapid large breaths/increased rate worsen auto-PEEP and venous return. Alarm = problem, not cause.

Disposition: ICU monitoring; reassess pressure, gases, mechanics, circulation after each change; handover mechanism and response.

Main chapter

Remember three compartments: open/protected airway, adequate oxygenation, adequate ventilation/CO2 removal.

Anatomy guides decision: larynx obstruction; alveoli gas exchange; respiratory muscles/central drive ventilation.

Positive pressure opens alveoli but can reduce venous return; monitor pressure.

Oxygen: usually 94–98%; CO2 retention risk 88–92%. Do not delay in severe hypoxia.

COPD with respiratory acidosis: BTS 2016: pH <7.35, PaCO2 >6.5 kPa after medical therapy → NIV first-line with monitoring/escalation.

ARDS: VT 4–8 mL/kg PBW, plateau <30 cm H2O; use predicted body weight.

Asthma: salbutamol 5 mg nebulized is bronchodilator, not instead of epinephrine for anaphylaxis.

Do not ignore: worsening ventilation, changing consciousness, vomiting/aspiration, hypotension after ventilation.

Before advanced support: airway failure plan? reversible cause? started? CICO = oxygenation failure; do not wait for paralysis reversal.

Paralysis ≠ sedation; prepare sedation and NM blockers. Ketamine does not replace sedation.

PBW from height/sex; 170 cm → VT 396 or 369 mL at 6 mL/kg, not actual weight.

Auto-PEEP may need brief disconnection; no single preset fits ARDS and air trapping.

Severe acidosis: preserve compensation, especially salicylate; do not assume normal PaCO2 or harmful ventilation.

Post-tube: capnography, sedation/analgesia, pressure support; hypotension needs cause assessment.

Chapter 04 Shock and Hemodynamic Reassessment
Key points

Shock = perfusion failure; BP may be normal initially. Link BP with consciousness, skin, urine output, lactate, response.

Four types: hypovolemic, distributive, obstructive, cardiogenic; can combine.

Body needs oxygen-carrying blood; fluids do not replace Hb in hemorrhage.

Do not repeat fluids automatically; reassess perfusion and breathing.

Septic shock: 30 mL/kg within 3 h is conditional SSC recommendation with reassessment.

MAP target 65 mmHg; in ≥65 years, 60–65 mmHg may be considered in individualized guidance.

Norepinephrine: peripheral use acceptable when monitored; starting example 8–12 micrograms/min per concentration/protocol; not fixed IV push.

Anaphylaxis: epinephrine 0.5 mg IM (1 mg/mL), repeat after 5 min; inhaled route does not replace.

Do not wait for imaging to control bleeding/obstruction; treat cause/source control.

High suspicion infection/shock: antimicrobials immediately, ideally within 1 h. Possible sepsis without shock: rapid assessment within 3 h if concern persists.

Read variable before formula: MAP may mean mean airway pressure in IOI or mean arterial pressure in CPP/SVR; write symbol/unit first.

POCUS answers a specific question: motion ≠ ROSC; effusion ≠ tamponade.

Neurogenic vs spinal shock; in trauma hypotension, think bleeding and reassess perfusion.

Chapter 05 Anaphylaxis Recognition Resuscitation and Safe Disposition
Key points

Recognize anaphylaxis if airway/breathing/circulation threat after likely exposure, or sudden hypotension with skin signs; rash may be absent.

Mechanism signs: laryngeal edema → voice change/stridor; bronchospasm → wheeze; vasodilation/leak → hypotension.

First drug: epinephrine IM, do not wait for antihistamine/steroid. Adult 0.5 mg IM (1 mg/mL = 0.5 mL); repeat after 5 min if severe features persist.

Weight-based ASCIA: 0.01 mg/kg up to 0.5 mg; check product/concentration; auto-injector dose ≠ infusion table. Examples: 20 kg → 0.20 mg/0.20 mL; 30 kg → 0.30 mg/0.30 mL.

Persistent breathing/circulation problem after 2 doses = refractory anaphylaxis pathway; ICU/resuscitation expertise.

Specialist infusion example: 1 mg in 100 mL NaCl 0.9%, initial 0.5 mL/kg/h with titration/monitoring; do not mix with IM concentration.

Fluids: UK adult initial example 500–1,000 mL bolus; ASCIA child 10–20 mL/kg; reassess.

Glucagon in refractory anaphylaxis on beta-blockers: RCUK 2021 adult 1 mg; child 20–30 micrograms/kg; confirm local protocol.

Salbutamol 5 mg nebulized is adjunct bronchodilator, not replacement for epinephrine. Steroids not first-line and do not reliably prevent biphasic reaction.

Observation UK: 2 h if complete resolution; ≥6 h after resolution with two doses or previous reaction; ≥12 h for higher risk factors; apply local protocol.

Discharge: plan, education, epinephrine autoinjectors, allergy follow-up, trigger avoidance.

Prescription identity: specify drug, indication, age, route, concentration, dose in mg and volume in mL; 0.5 mg IM for anaphylaxis ≠ 1 mg IV for cardiac arrest.

Chapter 06 Sepsis and Early Infection Decisions
Key points

Core: infection + organ dysfunction; look for consciousness, breathing, urine, perfusion; fever not required.

Pathophysiology: vasodilation, capillary leak, microcirculatory disturbance, ± myocardial depression.

Source determines intervention: abscess, urinary obstruction, biliary, necrotizing soft tissue, device.

Sepsis is not a diagnosis; do not use qSOFA alone to rule out; normal lactate does not rule out.

Shock/probable sepsis: antimicrobials immediately, ideally within 1 h (SSC 2026). Possible sepsis without shock: rapid workup/treatment within 3 h if concern persists.

Fluids: ≥30 mL/kg within first 3 h in specific context, conditional, individualized, reassessed.

MAP: initial general target 65 mmHg; age ≥65 years initial suggestion 60–65 mmHg.

Norepinephrine first vasopressor for septic shock; concentration/titration per local protocol.

No single universal antimicrobial dose; source, resistance, kidney function change plan.

Three errors: delaying needed antimicrobial, delaying source control, repeating fluids despite congestion.

Chapter 07 Fluids Electrolytes and Acid Base Emergencies
Key points

Separate rescue, maintenance, replacement; maintenance 25–30 mL/kg/day, may reduce 20–25 mL/kg/day in frailty/heart/kidney disease.

Hyperkalemia: protect heart → shift into cells → remove from body; different timing/functions.

Severe UKKA ≥6.5 mmol/L: calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL over 10 min, or calcium chloride 10% 10 mL over 5 min depending access.

Insulin-glucose: 10 units with 25 g glucose; verify units/concentration; monitor glucose ≥6 h, longer if risk persists.

Salbutamol for hyperkalemia: 10–20 mg nebulized; adjunct, not definitive alone in severe cases.

Hyponatremia: IV push not universal; correction rate ≤10 mmol/L/h, peripheral concentration ≤40 mmol/L; correct magnesium.

Symptomatic severe hyponatremia example: 150 mL 3% over 20 min, reassess; initial rise ~5 mmol/L, not full normalization.

Correction limits: 10 mmol/L first day, then 8 mmol/L/day; high ODS risk → slower specialist-guided.

Symptomatic hypocalcemia: calcium gluconate 10–20 mL of 10% over 10 min with monitoring; do not mix with bicarbonate.

Magnesium deficiency can provoke arrhythmia/torsades; correct cause.

Anion gap: AG = Na − (Cl + HCO3).

Expected compensatory PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO3) + 8 mmHg, ±2 mmHg.

Memorization errors: calcium does not remove potassium; normal number does not rule out mixed disorder; faster correction not always better; corrected Na and correction limit are different.

Chapter 08 Analgesia Sedation and Medication Safety
Key points

Ask function, not just number: can the patient move, breathe, tolerate examination?

Somatic pain more localized; visceral may be referred; neuropathic burning/electric and may persist.

Paracetamol adult: 1 g every 6 h, max 4 g/24 h from all products; lower in low weight/risk.

NSAIDs: e.g. ibuprofen 400 mg then 250 mg every 6–8 h, or naproxen; avoid in kidney injury, bleeding, heart disease; do not combine NSAIDs.

Morphine: reference 0.1 mg/kg IV titrated; not fixed rapid bolus; adjust kidney/age and monitor.

Fentanyl: reference 1 microgram/kg, not mg/kg; check product/route.

Ketamine analgesia/sedation 0.1–0.3 mg/kg IV (RCEM 2024); not airway strategy; does not guarantee airway patency.

Sedation ≠ analgesia; delirium/agitation different mechanisms. Sedation requires clear roles, monitoring consciousness, breathing, BP, capnography, rescue capability, fasting per plan.

Normal saturation on oxygen does not rule out opioid/sedative hypoventilation.

Before discharge: review drug/dose/duration, interactions, driving/sedation instructions.

Urgency vs fasting: assess why procedure now and danger of delay; do not automatically delay emergency care for fasting.

Renal dysfunction changes titration; morphine to hydromorphone still risks accumulation/respiratory depression.

Recovery is clinical: procedure end ≠ danger end; discharge depends on recovery, breathing, hemodynamics, new oxygen need, companion plan.

UK methoxyflurane/Penthrox update: prescription-only; UK guideline includes children ≥6 years in specific indications; check local licensing/contraindications.

Prescription order: indication → route → mg or mcg → concentration. Do not mix AHA and RCUK in one prescription.

Chapter 09 Chest Pain and Acute Coronary Syndromes
Key points

Check stability and dangerous alternatives first; don’t let automatic “mono” order precede thinking of dissection/bleeding.

ECG within 10 min, trained interpretation; repeat if needed. Do not wait for troponin if reperfusion indicated.

Elevated troponin = myocardial injury needing interpretation, not automatically clot; MI requires ischemia context.

0/1 or 0/2 h pathways depend on device/algorithm conditions; do not use universally or mix injury thresholds with diagnostic thresholds.

Aspirin: 162–325 mg chewed, then 75–100 mg daily in appropriate patient.

Ticagrelor: load 180 mg, then 90 mg twice daily in appropriate pathway without thrombolysis. Clopidogrel without lysis: load 300 or 600 mg, then 75 mg daily; choice by age/plan.

ACSL initial: UFH 60 units/kg max 4,000, then 12 units/kg/h max start 1,000; aPTT targets per initial/PCI context; PCI heparin 1,000 units and ACT separate.

Enoxaparin example 1 mg/kg SC every 12 h; every 24 h if CrCl <30 mL/min; check kidney.

Tirofiban as given: 0.3–0.4 mg every 5 min up to 3 doses if no allergy; monitor BP/route/unit; example infusion 10 micrograms/min.

Contraindications: hypotension, suspected aortic dissection, recent PDE5 inhibitor; check timing by drug.

Atorvastatin 80 mg high-intensity example; interactions/kidney/ACS not equivalent.

Angiography/reperfusion time-dependent; 120 min important decision point.

Deterioration/new murmur after MI: think mechanical complication, urgent cardiology/surgery.

HEART 5 elements × 2, score 0–3: 10; not substitute for clinical decision/troponin. Rest pain/peptic ulcer ≠ UA.

Heartburn presentation: choosing gastric drug before assessing ischemia in at-risk patient is trap.

Prinzmetal/vasospastic angina: history guides but does not replace ischemia assessment.

ECG: review Wellens, de Winter, posterior changes; Sgarbossa needs baseline, not universal alternative.

DAPT table with OAC risk and initial care.

Chapter 10 Acute Heart Failure Cardiogenic Shock and Valve Emergencies
Key points

Start with two axes: congestion and perfusion; can coexist; BP may be preserved in cardiogenic shock.

Left heart congestion → lungs; right heart congestion → veins and reduced LV filling. Understand preload before diuretic/fluid/inotrope.

Echo clarifies ventricles, valves, pericardium, complications; ejection fraction alone does not exclude significant failure.

Search precipitant: ischemia, arrhythmia, pressure, infection, renal, drugs, mechanical lesion.

Furosemide: example 40 mg IV in ESC 2026 context if no prior diuretic, then titrate to response/kidney; usual dose on chronic diuretic needs review.

Nitrate in ACS with pulmonary edema/persistent symptoms: example start 10 micrograms/min with titration/monitoring; not 10 mg/min or universal high dose.

Positive pressure improves lung congestion and reduces venous return; monitor BP/perfusion with breathing.

Vasopressor supports pressure; inotrope supports contractility; neither fixes mechanical cause.

Morphine not routine for pulmonary edema; needs indication and monitoring sedation/respiratory status.

Acute regurgitation with edema/shock: urgent echo and specialist input even if exam not low.

Severe aortic stenosis: avoid sudden preload/afterload drop; tachycardia poorly tolerated.

Prosthetic valve + dyspnea/shock/embolic event: think thrombosis, infection, dysfunction, dehiscence; specialist team decides.

Exam trap: improvement after diuretic/nitrate ≠ adequate perfusion/BP assessment.

Dressler = post-MI immune pericarditis, often weeks later; instability → exclude rupture/tamponade first.

Infective endocarditis Duke criteria: memorize major/minor, name version, do not mix old/new.

Chapter 11 Arrhythmia Management and Syncope
Key points

Start: pulse present? perfusion stable? shock/hypoxia/edema linked to rhythm → urgent stabilization.

Wide irregular tachycardia: think VT; also AF with pre-excitation and polymorphic VT.

Adenosine UK selected sequence: 6 → 12 → 18 mg only in eligible monitored patient; avoid routine with wheeze/asthma history.

Atropine RCUK: 500 mcg, repeat every 3–5 min up to 3 mg when appropriate; pacing may matter more than repeating drug.

Torsades: stop correctable QT cause, treat electrolytes; know pacing/dose from primary pathway.

AF >24 h or unknown onset: think stroke protection before elective cardioversion; common 3 weeks before and 4 weeks after, specialist exceptions.

Syncope with exertion, no warning, family sudden cardiac death, abnormal ECG: do not reassure just because consciousness returned.

PAC/PVC: syncope/structural heart disease/abnormal ECG removes automatic reassurance; not every PVC needs amiodarone, not every PAC needs anticoagulation.

Orthostatic hypotension: name definition/pathway; distinguish delayed form from classic definition.

Chapter 12 Aortic and Peripheral Vascular Emergencies
Key points

Sudden pain + neuro deficit/syncope/pulse difference: include aortic disease in life-threatening diagnoses.

Type A involves ascending aorta → usually urgent surgery. Type B starts distal to left subclavian; often medical/surveillance unless complicated.

Aortic dissection with high BP: HR 60–80/min, SBP <120 mmHg or lowest pressure preserving perfusion; do not apply blindly in shock.

AAA rupture: blood and definitive treatment fast; avoid excessive fluids; ultrasound does not rule out rupture.

Cold painful limb + sensory/motor deficit: urgent revascularization assessment; incomplete signs do not justify delay.

Acute arterial occlusion: do not automatically use MI heparin loading; watch bleeding/compartment/HIT.

Post-reperfusion: monitor potassium, acidosis, kidney, compartment syndrome; pulse does not exclude compartment.

High BP without acute organ injury: no rapid IV drop for number alone; recheck and plan follow-up.

Chronic PAD vs acute ischemia: clinical classification matters more than vague “PVD”; organ threat defines emergency; discharge needs plan.

Chapter 13 Pulmonary Embolism and Venous Thrombosis
Key points

Virchow triad: stasis, vascular injury, hypercoagulability. PE can cause right heart failure/shock.

PERC only very low pretest probability; positive item not proof. Wells >4 = PE likely (two-level).

Eligible >50 years with FEU: age-adjusted D-dimer = age × 10 ng/mL; verify lab unit.

Apixaban adult: 10 mg twice daily ×7 days, then 5 mg twice daily; do not apply AF dose-reduction rule automatically.

Negative proximal ultrasound + positive D-dimer may need repeat in 6–8 days per NICE; treatment usually ≥3 months then review duration.

Unstable patient: resuscitation pathway; not oral outpatient prescription.

As given: memorize length/distance 5 cm × 3 cm × 45 cm; fondaparinux 2.5 mg daily for eligible DVT context; prophylaxis dose ≠ treatment dose.

PE categories A–E: new classification includes subgroups; “D or E needs catheter” is too simple. Decide by severity, deterioration, bleeding risk, center experience; RV strain alone insufficient.

PE: classify A–E first; C–E need admission per 2026 framework. “No” to all Hestia items only supports outpatient evaluation, not guaranteed discharge.

Chapter 14 Asthma COPD and Acute Wheeze
Key points

Silent chest + fatigue/altered consciousness = danger. Normal/rising PaCO2 in persistent asthma suggests exhaustion, not improvement.

Asthma target in GINA 2026: 92–95%. COPD with CO2 retention risk: usually start 88–92% with blood gas assessment.

Moderate asthma example: salbutamol 4–6 puffs (100 mcg/puff) or 2.5 mg nebulized; severe: 6–10 puffs or 5 mg nebulized with urgent review/reassessment.

Asthma steroids: prednisolone 40–50 mg daily ×5–7 days. Severe selected non-responder: magnesium 2 g over 20 min.

COPD steroids: prednisone equivalent 40 mg daily ×5 days; NICE prednisolone 30 mg ×5 days; choose local protocol.

COPD NIV after persistent acidosis despite treatment: pH <7.35, PaCO2 >6.5 kPa, with monitoring/escalation.

No asthma discharge without ICS plan and follow-up. No routine antibiotics for severe obstruction without infection evidence.

Obstruction needs oxygen during assessment; do not withhold from NIV candidate without clear escalation limits.

Chapter 15 Pneumonia Pleural Disease and Hemoptysis
Key points

Bronchitis often viral; sputum color does not mandate antibiotic. Legionella needs urine antigen/specific test and effective coverage. Mycoplasma lacks cell wall; beta-lactam alone inadequate.

Older adults: pneumonia may present with confusion and no fever; mental status/tachypnea are clues.

CRB65: confusion, RR ≥30/min, SBP <90 or DBP ≤60 mmHg, age ≥65; one point each. CURB65 adds urea >7 mmol/L.

Low-severity CAP example: amoxicillin 500 mg TID ×5 days. Alternative: doxycycline 200 mg day 1, then 100 mg daily ×4 days; review allergy/kidney/pregnancy/stability.

Do not fix “5 days” without conditions; complications/non-response need another plan; steroids only selected severe cases.

Light criteria: protein >0.5, LDH >0.6, or LDH >2/3 upper normal serum; one criterion enough.

Pus or pH ≤7.2 with pleural infection → assess drainage safety.

Tension pneumothorax is physiologic threat; do not delay rescue for imaging.

Lung cancer emergency: resuscitation/complication treatment can precede staging.

Influenza early treatment preference does not mean late treatment is never useful.

TB: isolate then test; one negative smear does not exclude.

Viral/fungal pneumonia: remember host/exposure/test, not just names; positive sample may not equal invasive disease.

HIV CMV pneumonitis: positive BAL PCR alone insufficient; correlate clinically and exclude other causes.

Hemoptysis: anatomical/physiologic severity matters more than single clot size.

Chapter 16 Altered Consciousness Delirium and Coma
Key points

Priorities: breathing, circulation, glucose, then find cause. Oxygenation does not guarantee adequate ventilation.

New/fluctuating delirium with attention deficit may be quiet; dementia does not justify sudden deterioration.

Suspected Wernicke per SPS pathway: thiamine 300–500 mg IV TID ×3–5 days with daily review; do not delay glucose if low.

After naloxone, respiratory depression may recur; after seizure, non-convulsive activity may persist and need EEG.

Normal CT does not exclude stroke/encephalitis; focal signs or ongoing deterioration need escalation.

GCS describes/trends; not diagnosis or single disposition. Calm patient does not mean safe.

TGA is diagnosis after excluding alternatives; not just repeated questions. MRI best for stroke mimic; early negative does not rule out TGA.

Chapter 17 Stroke Transient Ischemia and Intracranial Hemorrhage
Key points

Last known well time, glucose, rapid imaging, stroke team; do not forget posterior circulation.

Eligible thrombolysis: tenecteplase 0.25 mg/kg max 25 mg single IV; not substitute for risk assessment.

Alteplase: 0.9 mg/kg max 90 mg; 10% bolus, rest over 60 min.

Before IVT selected pathway: BP <185/110 mmHg; do not apply ICH targets to every ischemic stroke.

4.5 h window not the only option; selected cases need advanced imaging/thrombectomy up to 24 h within criteria.

NICE: assess within 24 h, aspirin 300 mg daily as given; do not use ABCD2 to delay assessment.

ICH selected: smooth SBP lowering toward 140 mmHg, avoid <130 per mentioned framework; reversal depends on anticoagulant, not routine for normal INR.

Eligibility not clock alone: late/unknown onset need imaging/specialist pathway; low NIHSS may still have functional deficit.

Post-reperfusion BP is separate: post-EVT/IVT target ≠ pre-lysis or ICH target; do not routinely chase SBP <140.

Locked-in: test eye commands; no speech/movement does not prove unconsciousness.

Early nimodipine improves outcomes/reduces delayed ischemia after aneurysmal SAH; does not replace securing aneurysm.

Chapter 18 Seizures and Status Epilepticus
Key points

5 min continuous seizure or recurrent seizures without consciousness recovery = status epilepticus; do not wait 30 min.

Adult example: lorazepam 4 mg IV over 2 min; monitor breathing/consciousness; repeat per pathway often after 5–10 min if ongoing.

Second-line loading example: levetiracetam 60 mg/kg IV max 4500 mg over 10 min; loading ≠ maintenance.

Failure of benzodiazepine + appropriate second drug → urgent escalation, ICU/EEG, not open sedative repeats.

Non-recovery may be non-convulsive status or other cause; after status, low consciousness not just postictal/sedation.

Treat cause in parallel: glucose, electrolytes, infection, withdrawal, pregnancy, trauma; do not delay follow-up/medication plan.

Chapter 19 Headache CNS Infection and Raised Intracranial Pressure
Key points

Sudden thunderclap headache needs assessment even if CT within 6 h is normal; differentiate SAH/xanthochromia, meningitis vs encephalitis.

Meningitis: do not delay antibiotics for CT/LP in delayed presentations. GCS ≤9, focal signs, abnormal pupils/new seizures → stabilize and safe imaging/LP review first.

Adult meningitis example: ceftriaxone 2 g IV every 12 h; add Listeria coverage when risk.

HSV encephalitis example: aciclovir 10 mg/kg IV every 8 h with kidney/weight/fluid monitoring.

Dexamethasone for meningitis when indicated: 10 mg every 6 h up to 4 days; review cause; do not delay antibiotics.

Migraine example: ibuprofen 400 mg + metoclopramide 10 mg after excluding dangerous causes; oxygen 12 L/min by appropriate mask per pathway.

Low consciousness/new abnormal pupil/extension posture: think raised ICP/herniation; maintain perfusion and call help; steroids/hyperventilation not universal treatment.

Tension-type headache: bilateral pressing, not worsened by routine activity; do not rely on “tension” label alone.

Cluster headache: unilateral severe with autonomic features; diagnose, exclude danger, treat attack then prophylaxis.

HSV encephalitis: 10 mg/kg IV every 8 h with normal kidney; adjust renal; at least 1 h per dose as given.

Chapter 20 Vertigo Weakness and Spinal Cord Emergencies
Key points

Bell palsy is peripheral pattern after excluding alternatives; forehead alone does not settle. Steroid for eligible ≥16 years within 72 h; eye protection not delayed.

Ask timing/triggers: continuous vertigo not always central; short positional episodes may be peripheral.

HINTS for continuous AVS with nystagmus by trained examiner; not general dizziness test. Negative CT does not rule out posterior stroke.

Dysphagia/hoarseness/neck weakness may precede hypoxia in muscle/nerve disease; direction/protection more important than single finding.

GBS may threaten breathing/bulbar function; steroids are not immune treatment. Myasthenia needs trigger/drug review and crisis assessment.

Back pain + urinary/bowel/sexual/sensory change: urgent cauda equina assessment; normal post-void residual does not exclude.

MSCC with neuro signs per NICE: dexamethasone 16 mg promptly then 16 mg daily until final plan; MRI ASAP and within 24 h, sooner if deterioration.

Do not generalize steroid dose to every cord swelling: tumor, infection, infarction, trauma differ.

Labyrinthitis: start with acute vestibular pattern, hearing, gait; not diagnosis of exclusion.

Herniated disk/radiculopathy: documented neuro exam > “SLR positive” alone.

GBS/myasthenia: bulbar function and serial respiratory measurements > single number.

CRPS/CTS/cervical root are different patterns; examine strength/distribution/sensory changes before naming pain.

Chapter 21 Agitation, psychosis, suicide risk and capacity
Key points

Panic attack ≠ panic disorder; exclude organic cause, explain, follow-up/treatment plan; do not make benzodiazepine a permanent prescription.

Sequence: secure place → assess breathing/circulation/glucose → verbal calming → search cause → medication/monitoring/disposition.

Delirium = fluctuating attention/awareness; look for disease/drug/withdrawal; do not stop at psychiatric history.

After sedation: monitor at least hourly, every 15 min in higher risk per conditions; may need continuous monitoring.

No universal sedation dose; depends cause/patient/protocol; review cumulative drugs and respiratory depression risk.

Suicide risk: intent, plan, means, previous attempt, support, safety plan; do not use SAD PERSONS alone for disposition.

Capacity is decision-specific now, not global judgment; no discharge before treating medical danger and completing necessary assessment.

GAD: ask worry content/duration/impact/triggers, not just tension.

MDD: OSCE assess safety, alternatives, self-care capacity.

PTSD: memorize time groups; no universal “after one month” rule.

Schizophrenia spectrum: negative/disorganized symptoms alone insufficient; clarify core symptoms.

Agitation: cause → treatment → monitoring → reassess; calm is not end of exam.

Chapter 22 Acute abdominal pain and surgical red flags
Key points

Four don’t-miss groups: bleeding/vascular rupture, ischemia, perforation, strangulating obstruction; add ectopic pregnancy if pregnancy possible.

Pain out of proportion → vascular insufficiency; do not wait for lactate or peritoneal signs.

Acute abdomen plan: resuscitation, analgesia, repeated focused exam, targeted imaging, early surgical consult when picture changes.

Paracetamol adult: 1 g, interval ≥4 h, max 4 doses/day; lower threshold with risk factors.

Do not forget extra-abdominal causes; urinalysis/simple score/simple image should not close diagnosis.

Discharge after reassessment with clear warnings/follow-up, not just because analgesic worked.

Chapter 23 Gastrointestinal bleeding and esophageal emergencies
Key points

Memorize: resuscitation → severity → blood when needed → targeted therapy → endoscopy/intervention to stop source.

Stable non-cardiac upper GI bleed: transfusion threshold <7 g/dL, target often 7–9 g/dL; do not wait for number in shock.

Non-variceal upper bleed: endoscopy within 24 h after resuscitation. Suspected varices: endoscopy within 12 h after resuscitation + vasoactive drug + appropriate antibiotic.

High-risk ulcer after hemostasis: PPI 80 mg IV then 8 mg/h ×72 h example, or approved high-dose alternative.

Variceal bleeding: ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily up to 7 days per allergy/resistance/status.

Esophagus: no saliva reaching mouth = possible complete obstruction; severe pain after vomiting → think rupture; caustics = no vomiting/no neutralization.

GERD: antacid improvement does not rule out malignancy; chronic symptoms need follow-up.

Gastritis: name dangerous alternatives before labeling pain gastritis, especially older adults.

Nonbleeding peptic ulcer/H. pylori: test 4 weeks after antibiotics and 2 weeks off PPI with safe symptom plan.

Dysphagia is not automatically GERD: consider stricture/achalasia/cancer; evaluate first.

Chapter 24 Hepatobiliary and pancreatic emergencies
Key points

Biliary: cholecystitis, choledocholithiasis, cholangitis; cholangitis with organ failure needs ICU and urgent drainage.

Pancreatitis: 2 of 3 — typical pain, enzymes ≥3× ULN, characteristic imaging; do not use enzyme rise alone for severity.

Pancreatitis basics: directed fluids, analgesia, nutrition when tolerated; no prophylactic antibiotic in sterile pancreatitis; not every case needs urgent ERCP.

Acute liver failure: altered consciousness + coagulopathy after acute injury without cirrhosis; monitor glucose, transfer to center, early transplant assessment.

Ascites: PMN ≥250 cells/mm³ supports SBP; antibiotics/albumin/lactulose linked to diagnosis, organ function, protocol.

No single dose combines these diseases.

Acute hepatitis: falling enzymes with worsening consciousness/coagulation may mean liver failure, not improvement.

Chapter 25 Vomiting, diarrhea, obstruction and anorectal disease
Key points

Watery untreated diarrhea/fatty stool → think Giardia. UC continuous, bloody/mucous, urgency; Crohn skip lesions/transmural/fistula/abscess. Distension/toxicity → search toxic megacolon/abscess.

Vomiting/diarrhea: assess perfusion, glucose, electrolytes; choose antiemetic/rehydration by severity, not symptom name.

General adult resuscitation example: 500 mL crystalloid over <15 min, reassess; smaller if fluid overload risk.

Antiemetic example: ondansetron 4 mg PO/IV slowly per product; review QT, K, Mg, interactions.

Obstruction + constant pain/shock/ischemia signs = urgent surgery; normal stool/flatus does not exclude early/partial obstruction.

Bloody diarrhea/toxic colon: no random antimotility; antibiotic guided by cause.

Severe perianal/gluteal pain may be deep abscess/Fournier, not hemorrhoids.

Pilonidal disease: location essential diagnosis, not cosmetic.

Anal fistula/thrombosed hemorrhoids/anorectal differential: hemorrhoids do not exclude cancer/other bleeding source.

Small vs large bowel obstruction: delayed stool does not exclude partial/early obstruction.

Obstruction with ischemia risk does not wait for a memorized interval.

Chapter 26 Acute kidney injury, dialysis and rhabdomyolysis
Key points

IgA nephropathy close to pharyngitis; PSGN often delayed 2–4 weeks. Check BP, potassium, creatinine, pulmonary congestion; oliguria not automatic.

Adult AKI definition: creatinine rise 26 micromol/L in 48 h, or ≥50% in 7 days, or urine output <0.5 mL/kg/h for >6 h.

Three causes: hypoperfusion, intrarenal injury, obstruction; four dangers: hyperkalemia, acidosis, pulmonary edema, uremia.

Cardiac-toxic hyperkalemia: calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL or calcium chloride 10% 10 mL depending context.

Potassium shift: IV insulin 10 units + 25 g glucose with monitoring; salbutamol 10–20 mg nebulized adjunct not sole; monitor glucose ≥6 h.

Dialysis for dangerous refractory disturbances, not creatinine number alone. Fluids do not treat obstruction or anuric pulmonary edema.

Rhabdomyolysis: monitor CK, potassium, kidney; routine bicarbonate/mannitol not proven alone.

AKI: cause, severity, response — not FENa or creatinine alone.

Chapter 27 Urinary infection, stones, retention and genital emergencies
Key points

Three don’t-delay: infected obstruction, testicular torsion, Fournier; need cause removal, not just analgesic/antibiotic.

Adult pyelonephritis example: ceftriaxone 1–2 g daily per culture/resistance/allergy; nitrofurantoin does not treat pyelonephritis/prostatitis.

Stone expulsion may help selected distal 5–10 mm stones with alpha-blocker; not for infected obstruction or renal deterioration.

As given: painful persistent condition for 4 h or more = urgent urology; do not wait if perfusion threatened.

After relief of large retention: monitor diuresis, BP, electrolytes. Visible hematuria needs cause follow-up even on anticoagulation.

Testicular pain: strong clinical suspicion overrides waiting for Doppler; one exam does not exclude torsion.

Urethritis: mention NAAT, prevention, partners; not dose alone.

Urinary incontinence: leakage does not exclude retention; check post-void residual before drug.

Infection + obstruction: antibiotic plus urgent drainage, not stone-expulsion drug alone.

Chapter 28 Diabetes, hypoglycemia and hyperglycemic crises
Key points

DI loses water with dilute urine; treat perfusion then water with monitoring; do not stack DDAVP with uncontrolled replacement; recheck sodium usually every 4 h during resuscitation.

Thyroid storm: severe hypermetabolism + organ deterioration; do not wait for hormone number. Be cautious with beta-blocker in low-output heart failure.

Adult PTU example: load 500–1,000 mg, then 250 mg every 4 h supervised; iodine after antithyroid drug at least 1 h.

Thyroid storm hydrocortisone: 100 mg IV then 50 mg every 6 h.

Treat cause; monitor heart, glucose, urine. Myxedema coma: ventilatory support, passive warming, adrenal cover. IV levothyroxine load 200–400 micrograms, or 100–200 micrograms with risk factors; do not mix micrograms/mg.

Adrenal crisis: hydrocortisone 100 mg immediately, then 200 mg/24 h or 50 mg every 6 h, with saline and glucose when needed; potassium may not be high.

Sudden headache/visual disturbance + hypotension: think pituitary apoplexy plus other neuro causes; steroids not delayed if unstable.

Cushing: ask cortisol excess? then source?

Hyperparathyroidism: read calcium + PTH together; not all hypercalcemia is parathyroid.

Heat/cold intolerance: OSCE name likely cause, dangerous alternatives, discriminating tests.

Thyroiditis types vs storm: hyperthyroidism is state; storm is emergency; treatments differ.

Refeeding: intake, thiamine, K/Mg/Phos, fluids, monitoring; not phosphate alone.

Chapter 30 Trauma resuscitation, hemorrhage and transfusion
Key points

Mental order: catastrophic hemorrhage → airway → breathing → circulation → consciousness → exposure/warming; reassess after every change.

No severe brain injury: temporary SBP 80–90 mmHg until bleeding control. Severe TBI: avoid SBP <80 per local pathway.

TXA within 3 h: 1 g IV over 10 min, then 1 g over 8 h; check injury time/prior doses.

Ionized calcium target ~1.1–1.3 mmol/L; calcium chloride ≠ gluconate volume equivalence.

Fibrinogen ≤1.5 g/L with significant bleeding: consider 3–4 g concentrate or equivalent, reassess. Platelet targets >50, >100 in TBI.

Normal initial Hb and negative FAST do not exclude bleeding. Fluids/blood buy time; source control is definitive.

Catastrophic external hemorrhage in xABCDE: X reminds catastrophic bleeding may kill first; do not delay hemorrhage control for routine sequence.

Catastrophic hemorrhage and airway both immediate threats; negative FAST is not safety clearance.

Massive transfusion protocol with TEG/ROTEM as part; do not delay source control.

Chapter 31 Head face and spine trauma
Key points

Prevent secondary injury: oxygenation/perfusion, seizure control, pupil/consciousness monitoring. Deteriorating exam > reassurance from old image.

Hyperosmolar therapy needs current verification: mannitol 0.25–1 g/kg IV if not hypotensive, or 3% saline 250 mL over 30 min per context; not automatic universal recipe.

NEXUS five: midline pain, intoxication, altered mental status, neuro deficit, distracting injury; do not use only part.

Neuro deficit with normal CT may need MRI. No routine high-dose steroids for spinal cord injury; no prophylactic hyperventilation for head injury.

Eye: vision-threatening/painful proptosis needs urgent intervention; increasing swelling/hemorrhage/airway threat is emergency.

Sensitive doses/decisions in old editions need current verification.

Chapter 32 Thoracic abdominal and pelvic trauma
Key points

Chest: tension pneumothorax, hemothorax, flail chest, airway injury; treat clinical threat before waiting for signs/images.

Abdomen: negative FAST does not exclude hollow viscus/retroperitoneal injury. Unstable may need intervention before CT.

Pelvis: stabilize if indicated, resuscitate with blood, plan source control; do not repeat maneuver; bleeding may not be pelvic alone.

Urethra: blood at meatus or injury signs → evaluate before blind catheter; bladder may need special imaging.

Conservative solid organ injury = monitored plan, not automatic discharge; bowel/lung contusion may deteriorate later.

No single drug/disposition combines these injuries; local verification needed.

Chapter 33 Extremity injury and limb threatening conditions
Key points

Ask first: limb-threatening? open fracture? compartment? dislocation with skin/vessel/nerve compromise? These change priority.

Check perfusion and nerves before and after reduction/stabilization, during waiting/transfer.

ABI <0.9 in lower limb injury is warning; normal pulse does not exclude vascular injury.

Compartment syndrome can occur with palpable pulse; do not wait for paralysis/absent pulse; single compartment pressure not sole operative decision.

Open fracture: protection, prompt appropriate antibiotics, tetanus, surgery; dose/range per local protocol; no universal dose.

Do not miss scaphoid, posterior hip, posterior knee dislocation, Lisfranc, tendon injury; persistent deficit after negative image needs completion.

Ottawa knee: age 55, patella, fibular head, 90°, 4 steps; imaging rule, not all injuries.

Chapter 34 Wounds bites burns and soft tissue injury
Key points

Wound: function, perfusion, depth, contamination, foreign body before closure; antibiotic not substitute for cause removal.

Hand bite/clenched-fist over joint: think human/fight bite, check retained foreign body/joint penetration.

Deep burn may be painless; calculate partial/full thickness area; superficial redness not added to TBSA.

Old adult burn example: 4 mL × kg × %TBSA over 24 h; half in first 8 h, half over 16 h; study estimate needing current burn center confirmation.

Smoke in enclosed space/hoarseness/cough: assess inhalation and CO; usual oxygen does not exclude.

No prophylactic antibiotic for every burn; no random chemical neutralization; small important-site/toxic chemical burn may need urgent care.

Pressure ulcer/injury: if dead tissue covers depth, do not classify by surface edge alone.

Pressure injury safeguards: stage from exposed depth; do not guess depth under tissue injury; deep tissue injury ≠ Stage 1 and no numeric skin stage.

Bite: clean 10, dirty 5; TIG per series/wound/immunity; examine depth/joint/tendon; closure selective.

Chapter 35 Trauma in children pregnancy and older adults
Key points

Mother first: prepare immediately; coordinate resuscitative delivery at arrest site with goal completion within 5 min without delaying transfer.

Uterus at/above umbilicus in arrest: high-quality resuscitation, call obstetric/neonatal/airway teams, manual uterine displacement, prepare delivery immediately.

If no ROSC: trained team performs indicated resuscitative delivery at arrest location, aim within 5 min; do not delay for theater/fetal monitoring.

Critical errors: waiting until minute 4 to prepare; stopping indicated efforts just because 5 min passed.

Chapter 36 Febrile Neutropenia
Key points

No fever ≠ no sepsis; high fever ≠ necessarily bacterial sepsis. Examine consciousness, breathing, perfusion.

Parallel steps: resuscitation, rapid useful cultures, appropriate antibiotics, source search; source may not be found.

Adult examples needing current verification: cefepime 2 g IV every 8 h; meropenem 1 g IV every 8 h; piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV every 6 h.

Not equal alternatives for every infection: review site, kidney, allergy, resistance, ESBL risk; cefepime does not reliably cover anaerobes.

No fixed vancomycin table for all; no continued fluids despite congestion; do not delay source control because antibiotics started.

Doses confirm book but not claim 2026 update.

Carbapenem + valproate may lose seizure control; review drug plan, not just increase dose.

Chapter 37 Skin soft tissue and invasive infections
Key points

Spreading skin without collection ≠ abscess; abscess ≠ necrotizing infection; ask depth/source.

Examples needing current verification: cefazolin 500 mg IV QID, or clindamycin 300 mg QID in selected cases; review kidney/allergy/resistance.

Pain out of proportion, rapid spread, systemic toxicity, bullae, sensory loss = think necrotizing; normal appearance/gas does not exclude.

Necrotizing/Fournier: urgent surgery, broad antibiotics, resuscitation; do not let imaging/culture delay source control.

Drainage decision: uninfected ulcer ≠ automatic antibiotic; examine perfusion, foreign body, joint/tendon injury.

Old doses not 2026 protocol; severe cases need individualized specialist choice/monitoring.

Impetigo: do not touch every scaly rash; severe pain/systemic deterioration needs review.

Chapter 38 Immunocompromised patients HIV and transplantation
Key points

Ask immune deficit type, drugs, time since transplant, prophylaxis, prior organisms; infection signs may be blunted.

CD4 <200 cells/mm³ raises concern for life-threatening disease but does not diagnose current presentation.

Pneumocystis example: TMP-SMX 15–20 mg/kg/day divided TID ×21 days; review kidney, potassium, allergy, current protocol.

HIV-PJP steroid example with hypoxia: prednisone 40 mg BID ×5 days, then 40 mg daily ×5 days, then 20 mg daily ×11 days; source thresholds PO2 <70 or A-a gradient ≥35; not for every case.

Transplant: surgery, infection, rejection, drug toxicity; consult team early; do not stop all immunosuppression or start blind empirical treatment.

Post-stem-cell transplant rash/diarrhea: GVHD plus infection; plan change needs specialist team.

Chapter 39 Anemia bleeding anticoagulants and thrombosis
Key points

Aplastic anemia = three cell lines, not Hb only; neutropenic fever/bleeding are warning signs; definitive treatment is transplant/immunosuppression per case.

Separate cause: bleeding, hemolysis, production deficiency, sequestration, drugs; number alone does not determine treatment.

Examples need current verification: transfusion threshold ~7 g/dL in selected stable patient, not shock/active ischemia; Factor VIII 50 IU/kg for selected CNS bleed in severe hemophilia A only.

Warfarin major bleeding: slow IV vitamin K + factor replacement by INR; table per weight up to 100 kg; warfarin-specific. Dabigatran: idarucizumab 5 g IV per table.

TTP: thrombocytopenia + microangiopathic hemolysis + organ concern; do not wait for pentad. HIT can thrombose despite low platelets; stop heparin and substitute.

Suspected transfusion reaction: stop transfusion, ABC, verify identity, call blood bank; do not assume simple reaction; watch breathing/hemolysis/HIT.

Sickle cell + fever/hypoxia/new infiltrate = think acute chest syndrome, not simple pain crisis.

Polycythemia vera: post-bath itch is clue, not diagnostic proof.

Hemolytic anemia/hypercoagulable states: may need repeat G6PD after episode; single antibody may not diagnose APS.

TTP: do not wait for pentad or ADAMTS13; 10–20% as given is not definitive exclusion.

Transfusion reaction: stop → ABC → identity → blood bank; no automatic furosemide.

Chapter 40 Cancer emergencies and treatment complications
Key points

Don’t-delay emergencies: neutropenic infection, spinal cord compression, SVC syndrome with organ threat, hypercalcemia, tumor lysis/kidney, cardiac.

Neutropenic fever pathway: 38.3°C once or 38°C for 1 h, with ANC <500 expected within 48 h; antibiotic within 1 h; instability changes plan.

Antibiotic example: cefepime 2 g IV every 8 h for appropriate adult; review kidney/resistance/allergy; not discharge prescription for every cancer patient.

Cord compression: early MRI and consult; do not wait for bladder disorder; do not mix dexamethasone doses from different chapters.

Hypercalcemia examples need verification: zoledronic acid 4 mg IV over 15 min, or calcitonin 4 IU/kg SC/IM as start with later plan/kidney review.

TLS: K↑, phosphate↑, uric acid↑, Ca↓. Rasburicase contraindicated in G6PD deficiency; uric acid sample needs special cooling/analysis within 4 h.

Recent updates limited; old doses need current review.

Acute leukemia ALL/AML/APL: do not wait for very high WBC to suspect acute leukemia.

Lymphoma Hodgkin/non-Hodgkin/Burkitt: file pattern not enough; final histologic classification/staging essential.

Chapter 41 Travel, Vector-borne and Emerging Infections
Key points

Sequence: place + history + exposure → severity → exam → targeted treatment/follow-up.

Fever after malaria area: rapid assessment; one negative test may not suffice; prior prophylaxis does not exclude infection.

Bleeding, altered consciousness, hypoxia, low urine output = organ failure risk even if fever falls.

Before primaquine: check G6PD and pregnancy; do not memorize old dose as universal modern dose; confirm regional dose.

Do not wait for classic rash/proof to think vector-borne infection.

Distinguish Lyme prophylaxis from treatment course; not every tick exposure qualifies.

Chapter 42 Early Pregnancy Bleeding, Ectopic Pregnancy and Pelvic Pain
Key points

Start with pain: hemodynamic stability before pregnancy viability.

Shoulder pain/syncope + positive pregnancy test = think intra-abdominal bleeding, even with little vaginal bleeding.

Single hCG does not determine location or exclude rupture; link ultrasound to clinical course/follow-up.

About 48 h: common re-evaluation for stable unknown-location per local pathway; not reason to delay return with danger signs.

Anti-D needs current instruction verification.

Amenorrhea: assess sexual development and uterus presence; no single age rule.

Ovarian cysts: presence of flow does not equal excluding torsion.

Abnormal uterine bleeding/miscarriage: empty uterus is not proof of complete miscarriage without reliable prior context.

Confirmed miscarriage medication: do not use regimen before confirming diagnosis/location; not substitute for rescuing bleeding/infection; check contraindications/follow-up/urgent return.

Check age, event, allergy, product first; 300 mg ≠ 300 micrograms; early recommendations not automatically applied to lactation.

Chapter 43 Eclampsia and persistent severe hypertension
Key points

Late bleeding: no digital cervical exam before excluding placenta previa by appropriate pathway.

~160 systolic or 110 diastolic = severe BP needs urgent pathway; verify local continuation conditions.

NICE eclampsia: MgSO4 4 g IV over 5–15 min, then 1 g/h, continue 24 h after last seizure; monitor breathing, reflexes, urine, kidney.

Post-delivery example: oxytocin 10 units IM; units alone not enough.

PPH: uterine massage, drugs, transfusion; TXA 1 g IV within 10 min early, within 3 h of birth; do not delay until uterotonics fail.

PROM/PPROM: assess fluid, maternal/fetal status, gestational age; escalate appropriately.

Severe pregnancy hypertension needs rapid treatment; Mg for seizures is not substitute for BP treatment; dose requires kidney/respiratory monitoring.

Chapter 44 Gynecologic Infection, Bleeding and Sexual Assault
Key points

Painful persistent adnexal mass may be abscess even after fever settles; ultrasound then control and appropriate antibiotic; no deep massage/excessive aspiration.

Pelvic pain/bleeding: stability first, then pregnancy test when needed, then cause.

Fever + mass/severe pain = think abscess/surgical disease; antibiotics alone may be insufficient.

Outpatient PID example for suitable non-pregnant adult: ceftriaxone 500 mg IM + doxycycline 100 mg BID + metronidazole 500 mg BID as given.

Sexual assault: treat, separate consent, privacy, time-sensitive prophylaxis, follow-up/safety.

No single universal safe numeric dose for every case; context/fluids/current pathway matter.

Endometriosis: laparoscopy not mandatory first step for every patient; modern pathway details need review.

New acute pain not automatically dysmenorrhea; exclude ectopic/torsion per case; laparoscopy not required before appropriate symptom plan.

Chapter 45 The Critically Ill Infant and Child
Key points

Quiet/tired child may be closer to respiratory failure than strongly crying child.

Normal BP does not rule out shock; monitor consciousness, pulse, perfusion, urine.

Epinephrine IV/IO example: 0.01 mg/kg max 1 mg, concentration 0.1 mg/mL; not anaphylaxis IM dose.

Example 10 kg: 0.1 mg = 1 mL of specified concentration before concentration change.

Fluids by cause/response; check glucose quickly; verify dextrose dose with current pediatric pathway before use.

Chapter 46 Pediatric Fever, Infection and Respiratory Emergencies
Key points

Infant with apnea may be pertussis; example azithromycin 10 mg/kg PO once daily ×5 days; monitor breathing/feeding; cough may not disappear immediately.

Lethargic infant/poor feeding/temperature instability: think serious infection even without high fever.

Stridor with barking = croup; stridor + drooling/toxic appearance = different airway danger; choose per pathway.

Severe croup: dexamethasone max 12 mg in selected pathway; do not mix protocol dose limits.

Epinephrine here is nebulized for stridor; do not convert nebulized volume to IV push.

Monitor stridor recurrence after epinephrine; immediate improvement alone not discharge criterion.

Silent chest + fatigue/apnea/hypoxia = escalation, not reassurance.

RSV: correct old trap: palivizumab is prophylaxis, not vaccine or acute treatment.

Viral exanthems: remember parvovirus fetal anemia risk; start measles OSCE with isolation.

Infant fever and BRUE require full eligibility criteria, not one age number.

Chapter 47 Pediatric Abdominal, Dehydration and Metabolic Emergencies
Key points

Green vomiting = exclude obstruction; intermittent pain + distension/lethargy = think volvulus/malrotation.

Lethargy, convulsion, poor feeding = check glucose immediately.

Ondansetron example: PO 0.15 mg/kg max 8 mg; review age, QT, electrolytes.

DKA: planned fluids + insulin + potassium + monitoring; no insulin bolus.

CPS: insulin 0.1–0.05 unit/kg/h after 1 h fluids and K >3.0 mmol/L. Worsening consciousness → treat per cerebral injury protocol, not blind bolus.

Pediatric doses are not automatic miniature adult doses; check weight, concentration, route, upper limit.

Chapter 48 Pediatric Neurologic, Hematologic and Safeguarding Emergencies
Key points

About 5 min continuous seizure = start rescue pathway; do not wait for long historical definition.

Midazolam IN example: 0.2 mg/kg max 10 mg; review concentration/breathing.

After two appropriate benzodiazepine doses with ongoing seizure: escalate to next line, not open repeat same drug.

Sickle cell: fever, chest, brain, spleen — four danger points.

Story inconsistent with development/unexplained injury = safeguarding; assess harm, protection plan, document safety before discharge.

Simple febrile seizure: age 6–60 months, generalized <15 min, once in 24 h; assess meningeal signs/source. Do not wait 15 min for ongoing convulsion.

Chapter 49 Older Adults, Frailty and Atypical Presentations
Key points

Always ask: what is baseline? what is new change? who confirms history?

New attention/confusion in older adult: may be delirium; dementia does not exclude new acute medical cause.

Fall = search injury + medical cause together.

Before prescribing: kidney, interactions, sedatives, antibiotics, opioids, diuretics, polypharmacy.

No single dose ratio for older adults; link dose to drug, function, response.

Discharge decision combines medical stability, mobility, understanding, support, safety.

Chapter 50 Poisoning Assessment, Toxidromes and Decontamination Decisions
Key points

Poisoning: team safety → breathing, circulation, glucose, temperature → substance/shape/time → treatment/monitoring.

Anticholinergic = dry; cholinergic = wet secretions; serotonin = clonus/hyperreflexia. Think patterns, not one drug.

Naloxone IV adult: start 0.1–0.4 mg titrated to breathing; may repeat/monitor; not child dose.

Activated charcoal selective; does not effectively treat metals, alcohols, caustics; no routine vomiting induction; airway protection needed.

Negative poison screen does not exclude danger; improvement after antidote does not exclude recurrent toxicity. Monitoring duration by poison, no universal number.

Co-ingestion does not exclude monitoring plan; name plan, not just antidote name.

Chapter 51 Analgesic Sedative Psychiatric and Cardiovascular Overdoses
OSCE sodium-channel blocker poisoning

Poisoning: treat ABC, seizures, ECG, cardiac toxicity, monitor response; do not rely on poison name alone.

Wide QRS + hypotension after poisoning: resuscitate and assess sodium-channel blockade; check glucose/ECG.

First minute: monitoring/ventilation as indicated, glucose, access, 12-lead ECG; ask substance, formulation, co-ingestants.

Use chapter bicarbonate pathway when indicated with repeat ECG, gases, sodium, potassium; treat clinical toxicity, not screening assay alone.

If perfusion/QRS worsens: reassess diagnosis/delivery/targets; escalate specialist rescue/critical care.

Trap: bicarbonate is not for every acidosis; random antiarrhythmic/over-alkalinization harms.

Disposition: significant cardiotoxicity to monitored critical care; handover serial ECG, seizures, hemodynamics, treatments, biochemical trends.

Main chapter

Acetaminophen: level at 4 h or later in acute known exposure; nomogram not for repeated/unknown-time exposure.

NAC IV example: 150 mg/kg over 1 h, then 50 mg/kg over 4 h, then 100 mg/kg over 21 h as given, with monitoring/stop criteria.

Salicylates: do not lose respiratory compensation; monitor acidosis, potassium, urinary alkalinization example 2 mEq/kg needing monitoring.

Cardiac poisoning selected example: insulin 1 unit/kg then 1 unit/kg/h is specialist rescue, not routine DKA.

Antidote dose alone insufficient: route, concentration, monitoring, admission/observation plan.

Lithium: extracorporeal treatment not by level alone; renal failure with level 4, low consciousness, seizures, serious arrhythmia = urgent nephrology consult.

Antidote depends on substance/toxicity; Fab, lipid, high-dose insulin not universally correct for every toxic cardiac poisoning.

INH + seizures/acidosis: pyridoxine 5 g IV first with respiratory support; per poison center.

Valproate: monitor ammonia/consciousness; shock/cerebral edema = escalate without waiting for high number.

Chloroquine/quinine: do not assume lavage removes drug; support/toxins first.

Serotonin toxicity: cooling, support, sedation first; cyproheptadine adjunct does not delay severe rescue.

Chapter 52 Alcohol Stimulants Recreational Drugs and Withdrawal
Key points

Coma with alcohol: check glucose, breathing, trauma, infection, other substances before closing diagnosis.

Thiamine IV 100 mg or IM example is not a modern complete system for every patient; does not replace glucose when low.

Withdrawal + agitation/hallucination/severe instability = monitoring/escalation, not just sedative prescription.

Severe withdrawal under observation example: lorazepam 2 mg IV.

Toxic alcohol example: fomepizole 15 mg/kg IV; not every alcohol and not full system alone.

Fomepizole: 15 mg/kg, then 10 mg/kg every 12 h ×4 doses, then 15 mg/kg every 12 h; adjust by level/time.

Concentrated nicotine with child: small amount may matter; monitor neuro/respiratory; do not induce vomiting.

Suspected Wernicke: 300–500 mg IV TID per mentioned NHS pathway; prophylaxis and rescue differ; do not delay glucose.

Chapter 53 Gases Chemicals Metals and Envenomation
Key points

Normal oxygenation does not exclude CO or cellular oxygen utilization failure.

Adult example: hydroxocobalamin 5 g IV over 15 min; may repeat 5 g, total 10 g per specialist assessment.

Insecticides: secretions, respiratory weakness, muscle weakness; titrate atropine to respiratory response, not pupil dilation only.

Iron: elemental iron, source sample 4–6 h; charcoal ineffective; negative image does not guarantee safety.

Caustics: no vomiting/no neutralization. Animal toxins: appropriate antivenom for region/product, not universal foreign book dose.

Methylene blue: identify product first: PROVAYBLUE 5 mg/mL; review G6PD, serotonergic interaction, kidney before repeat.

Thallium: GI symptoms → neuropathic pain → late alopecia; Prussian blue with toxicology.

H2S: absence of smell is not safety; rescuer safety then oxygenation/ventilation; no proven antidote.

Bufo: digoxin-like cardiotoxicity; pain/pricking possible; warm water cautiously without burn; wound care.

Chapter 54 Heat Cold Drowning Diving and Electrical Injury
Key points

Hyperthermia + altered consciousness = heat stroke; cooling does not wait for tests.

<35°C = hypothermia; no resuscitation decision based on temperature alone.

38°C is rewarming stop point in source, not substitute for current pathway/monitoring.

Drowning = oxygenation, ventilation, lung monitoring; diving + neuro/chest symptoms = oxygen and urgent pressure consult.

Electrical = heart + deep muscles + nerves + rhabdomyolysis even with small skin mark.

Cold body, frozen tissue, diving dysrhythmias differ; do not combine numbers into one protocol.

Chapter 55 Acute Eye Ear Nose Throat and Dental Emergencies
Key points

Drooling/dysphagia + stridor = airway plan before imaging. Eyelid redness/swelling with ptosis suggests orbital involvement; mastoiditis signs need ENT + imaging/IV.

Red eye + pain/decreased vision/abnormal pupil = exclude vision-threatening disease.

Chemical burn: immediate irrigation + pH monitoring; do not stop with fixed routine; open globe needs shield, no pressure/tonometry.

Acute angle closure: firm eye important; acetazolamide 500 mg PO example in specialist plan; review contraindications and definitive laser.

Epistaxis TXA: 500 mg in 5 mL topically example; route matters.

Drooling + voice change/floor-of-mouth swelling = airway danger. Permanent tooth not reimplanted as given.

Acute laryngitis: ask swallowing/breathing before hoarseness severity.

Acute pharyngitis: secretions are not rapid GAS test; assess indications/alternatives.

Acute sinusitis: clinical pattern, not automatic diagnosis.

Allergic rhinitis: external signs not required; overuse decongestant can cause rebound.

Blepharitis: distinguish margin infection from eyelid mass/diffuse cellulitis; avoid random ocular steroid.

Conjunctivitis OSCE: visual acuity then exclude serious causes before naming inflammation.

Dacryoadenitis vs dacryocystitis: location key, final diagnosis needs context/exam.

AMD: know pattern; any acute vision loss needs appropriate assessment.

Auricular hematoma: not cosmetic only; protect cartilage/prevent recurrence.

TM perforation: safety linked to drug/concentration, not old name generalization.

Otitis media vs externa: location before treatment.

Peritonsillar/dental abscess: state danger, source, drainage, referral without irrelevant procedural detail.

Hypopyon: pus; differentiate term/diagnosis/escalation.

Follow-up by risk/type; chemical eye burn does not wait for routine interval.

Epistaxis: firm pressure on soft part ≥5–10 min, then source/intervention; escalate large/posterior bleed.

Chapter 56 Dermatology and Drug Eruptions
Key points

Night itch + mucosal involvement suggests scabies; permethrin 5% from 2 months, not every newborn. Tense itchy blisters in older adult → bullous pemphigoid; check infection/skin loss/dermatology.

Rash danger: breathing/circulation, severe pain, mucosa, skin detachment, fever, organ injury.

SJS <10%, overlap 10–30%, TEN >30% by classification; measure detached skin properly.

Stop suspected drug; support fluids/temperature/pain; eye/mucosa early evaluation. RMSF: doxycycline adult 100 mg every 12 h; child <45 kg 2.2 mg/kg every 12 h max 100 mg/dose.

No single steroid/immunosuppressant for every case; antihistamine does not replace anaphylaxis treatment.

Pediculosis/lice: do not mix scabies/lice products; verify application before switching.

Dermatitis/herpes zoster: old age limit for shingles not modern criterion; comparison table supports emergency decision.

DRESS may start before rash; fever/nodes/organ involvement after drug needs evaluation/escalation, not antihistamine only.

DRESS: RegiSCAR; severity by organs/stability; do not wait full score to stop suspected drug; slow steroid taper/long organ monitoring; skin improvement ≠ organ danger gone.

AGEP: acute pustulosis with neutrophilia; overlap with DRESS changes follow-up/treatment.

Chapter 57 Nontraumatic Musculoskeletal and Rheumatologic Emergencies
Key points

Hot joint = exclude infection; no fever/crystals present does not exclude septic arthritis.

Synovial WBC >50,000/μL increases doubt but no absolute cutoff separates infectious/non-infectious.

Low-dose colchicine example: 1.2 mg then 0.6 mg after 1 h, total 1.8 mg, review contraindications/interactions/repeat; no hourly dosing to toxicity; no steroid injection before excluding infection.

Allopurinol not absolutely forbidden if indicated with prophylaxis/follow-up.

Child limping with knee pain: examine hip; refusal to walk/fever/systemic illness = escalate.

Crystals do not exclude infection; synovial count is not single exclusion.

Chapter 58 Palliative Care Goals of Care and End of Life Emergencies
Key points

Palliative care = comfort consistent with goals, not abandoning care.

Define decision capacity, patient wishes, limits of each intervention, who follows implementation.

DNR ≠ no analgesia and not treatment for every reversible cause of breathlessness.

Morphine 0.05 mg/kg IV for dyspnea is selected source example; review frailty, kidney, prior doses, monitor ventilation.

No discharge without drug/support/communication plan; do not automatically apply foreign documents/wills locally.

Chapter 59 Abuse Safeguarding Forensic and Social Emergencies
Key points

Priorities: treat danger → privacy/consent → objective documentation → protection/follow-up.

Visible injury does not exclude abuse; inconsistent story alone does not prove it.

Consent for exam ≠ automatic consent for images/samples/information sharing.

No uniform prevention from old edition; no automatic foreign legal rules.

Safe discharge needs destination, support, follow-up plan; stable vitals alone insufficient.

Blood exposure: HBV immunity/vaccine; HIV urgent decision; HCV testing/follow-up, no fixed universal prophylaxis.

Safeguarding: treat, protect, consent, document; foreign law ≠ local policy.

Chapter 60 Disaster Mass Casualty and Prehospital Decisions
Key points

Disaster: safety → command/communication → triage → possible treatment → transport/distribution → reassessment.

START example: RR >30/min, capillary refill >2 s, or unable to follow simple command, within full system.

Do not transfer adult numbers to children, do not mix triage systems, do not remove triage based on color alone.

Destination by capacity/need/resources, not distance alone; transport team by deterioration risk.

Contamination ≠ irradiation; protection important; urgent contamination treatment continues in protected path.

Scene safety: team safety first; do not enter unsafe area without protection/training; request resources early.

Transport/handover: do not delay definitive treatment for unnecessary on-scene procedures; nearest hospital not always best; handover clinical course/response/residual risk, not just diagnosis.

START/SALT: know system before color; walking ability not final judgment.

Expectant ≠ dead; gray category resource-linked, not final judgment.

Operations: command/surge needs logistics/planning/staff/equipment/workflow; do not describe increased capacity without them.

CBRN exposure/contamination differ from decontamination; rescuer protection first; decontamination method depends material.

Radiation countermeasures: KI for thyroid, DTPA/Prussian blue for specific materials; do not choose antidote for the word “radiation” alone.

Blast: pressure, shrapnel, blast wave, other effects; ear alone does not judge lung injury. Oxygen duration/consumption/safety reserve, not pressure alone.

Jurisdiction/ceiling: do not present foreign laws as Jordanian; CPR does not mean no treatment; document capacity/explanation/alternatives/local protocol.

OSCE scene response: safety, command/resources, triage, lifesaving intervention, contamination control, distribution/transport, reassessment; explain decision instead of reciting names.

Chapter 61 Safe Disposition Handover Diagnostic Uncertainty and Follow Up
OSCE refusal and safe discharge

Safe discharge: capacity, understanding, alternatives, pending results, follow-up; paper alone insufficient.

Assess decision-specific capacity, reversible barriers, explain risk/options in understandable language.

Look for hypoxia, hypoglycemia, delirium, intoxication, pain, communication barriers; patient must understand, retain, weigh, communicate decision.

Ask patient to explain risk/plan in own words; explore practical alternatives without coercion.

Capacity impaired + serious immediate danger: senior advice and lawful local emergency pathway, least restrictive effective intervention.

Signing refusal does not prove capacity or discharge safety; disagreement ≠ incapacity; foreign legal tests not automatic Jordanian law.

Document clinical assessment, capacity reasoning, offered alternatives, advice, review plan; allocate ownership of pending results; red flags/return access.

Main chapter

Before discharge: diagnosis/uncertainty/trajectory, baseline/function, medication, support, follow-up.

Observation = goal, endpoint, escalation criterion, responsible person; not open waiting.

Dose/route/concentration/volume units differ; mg ≠ mL.

Pending result = responsible person + follow-up + communication + documented handover: what happened, what remains, what may deteriorate.

No discharge/refusal replaces patient understanding, capacity, safety plan; end of visit is clinical re-evaluation.

Classify error and what changes decision: information, diagnosis, order, number/unit, exception, interpretation, guideline copy, follow-up/discharge; then write why alternative wrong and when correct. Study method, not formal board tool.

Test performance needs denominator/context; NNT needs ARR, outcome, time.

Chapter 62 Diagnosis to Next Step and High-Yield Comparisons
Key points

Stabilization first: do not let examination delay treatment of respiratory failure, bleeding, life-threatening arrhythmia.

Synchronized cardioversion for regular unstable rhythm; polymorphic VT / pulseless VT / VF uses unsynchronized defibrillation per algorithm.

Oxygen saturation with mask/BVM/chest tube: don’t turn DOPES into a definitive diagnosis; treat deteriorating cause.

Tension pneumothorax: do not rely on asthma/COPD label. ARDS: PEEP/plateau; wheeze is not one diagnosis.

One negative sign (US, crepitus, pulse) does not exclude danger.

Acute urinary retention with tachycardia/agitation: consider opioid infection/anuria as given; torsion needs urgent surgical escalation.

Toxidromes: sodium-channel blockade → sodium-channel blocker; INH + seizure → pyridoxine; sulfonylurea/long-acting insulin → hypoglycemia, check glucose fast.

Toxic alcohols: absent osmolar gap does not exclude; no single equation/table replaces clinical course.

Unknown source + HIV sexual exposure: do not use occupational algorithm blindly; PEP within 72 h.

Suicide: individual assessment, not discharge by score alone; DNR ≠ no care.

Hidden clue: use to prioritize diagnosis, not definitive proof.
